          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The phrase denotes Jesus’ death-and-burial location or state: the grave/tomb, often broadened to Sheol or Hades, between death and resurrection.\",\"book_summary\":\"The phrase denotes Jerusalem, understood as the geographical and symbolic navel or heart of the land.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"The grave or realm of the dead.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Jerusalem, the center of the land.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"This is not merely a narrower account of the grave or Hades. The book reclassifies the phrase from a burial or underworld referent to a geographical city, thereby replacing the consensus’s central identification.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"In Matthew 12:40, the same expression cannot principally identify both Jesus’ grave or postmortem realm and Jerusalem in the same contextual sense. Accepting the book’s identification requires rejecting the consensus identification as the phrase’s intended referent.\",\"rationale\":\"The book’s Jerusalem reading is divergent rather than novel because all blind responses recognize Jerusalem, the land’s center, or earthly custody there as a minority or rare alternative. Nevertheless, that recognition does not make it a refinement: Jerusalem is a materially different referent from the tomb, grave, or Hades.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Ezekiel 5:5 and 38:12 support Jerusalem or its people being situated in the midst of lands or nations, but they do not call Jerusalem the “heart of the earth” or establish that Matthew 12:40 uses the phrase geographically. Matthew’s immediate Jonah analogy more directly suggests confinement associated with death and deliverance.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
